In article <1992Feb02.233145.4687@r-node.guild.org> sources@r-node.guild.org (Sources Login) writes:
>illegal redeclaration of sprintf at line 131.  Why?  How do I get around it?
>It crashes here, so it isn't as if I can just ignore it.

Go to line 131, and delete the misdeclaration of sprintf() on that line.  The
simplest correct way to declare sprintf() is through #include <stdio.h>, which
I presume has already been done.

If the collision is due to the return type (standard sprintf() returns an int,
some older versions return a pointer), and the code is actually using the
return value, then you'll have to fix those.  If the collision is merely due
to the fact that no non-prototype declaration is compatible with the true
variadic signature of sprintf(), then deleting the declaration (or enclosing
it in #if !defined(__STDC__)) should be enough.

